# Reclaim the disk every release leaves behind.
#
# A CX22 has 40 GB and a deploy pulls seven images tagged with a commit SHA, so
# the box accumulates a full image set per release and would fill in a couple
# of months of merges. A full disk on this box does not degrade gracefully:
# Postgres refuses writes, the audit insert that every recommendation depends
# on fails closed, and the first symptom is 500s rather than a disk alert.
#
# Two deliberate limits on what this removes:
#
#   * `--filter until=168h` keeps the last week of images. That is the window
#     in which a rollback might still want an older release's layers locally --
#     `deploy.sh --rollback` pulls them again in any case, but pulling seven
#     images during an incident is a slower rollback than reusing them.
#   * volumes are never pruned, by this unit or any other. `docker volume
#     prune` would take both Postgres data directories and the Redis AOF with
#     it the moment a deploy had the containers stopped, which is the single
#     most destructive command available on this box. There is no flag here
#     that can reach a volume, and that is the point.
#
# Images in use by a running container are never candidates, so the running
# release is safe regardless of its age.

[Unit]
Description=MovieLens weekly Docker image prune
Requires=docker.service
After=docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/opt/movielens
User=deploy
Group=deploy
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker image prune --all --force --filter until=168h
TimeoutStartSec=600
